rst drought he's ever witnessed  [Music]  the deeper inland the worst the drought  we drove by one homestead after another  deserted by families as the drought  claimed their livestock  and this is the the main homestead  and  probably they had children's here they  gave birth to children's here one two  three kids here  and so you only leave if you have to  yeah yeah that is  it's the worst  in the worst situation and they you know  that is the last thing they can do  living leaving their homes they had to  leave they're not they had no choice  truly they had no choice  outside one village we met the governor  of the region  its people desperately need help he told  us but the drought he says is being made  worse by the war in ukraine  it may look busy but in the only big  flower mill in somaliland they are  running out of grain  russia's blockade of ukraine means they  haven't had a shipment for three months  its silos are emptying and they may soon  have to stop grinding grain  maybe everybody will lose his job  also uh uh  somalia population how they will get  food in general this war affected us as  a company as a company we are affected  by this war  that's it  if the mill shuts down somaliland will  lose its main source of bread  it's hard to think of a more graphic  illustration of the impact of russia's  war in ukraine on developing nations one  of the world's poorest countries  literally running out of grain it's  silos standing empty while there is  millions of tons of grain stuck in  ukraine unable to get out  war and droughts now haunt somaliland  and the entire horn of africa  threatening a humanitarian disaster  unicef says without action the mass  death of children will be inevitable and  the people here know it's coming  the last of three wells in this village  to have any water left camel herders had  dire predictions  the drought is bad he says but worse is  on its way  dominic waghorn sky news somaliland  well let's get some more of today's  climate news for you now and the  european commission has proposed legally  binding targets to restore nature across  the european union in an attempt to  recover plunging wildlife populations  they'll also be individual targets to  help specific species and habitats for  example pollinators like bees and  farmland birds and to restore rivers  back to their natural courses  while the proposals would require eu  countries to restore nature to 20  of eu land by 2030.  the united states continues to face an  intense heat wave temperatures are  likely to reach nearly 40 degrees  celsius today in cities like atlanta and  charlotte as the heatwave moves  eastwards the unusually hot conditions  will likely remain until the end of the  month the extreme heats are likely to  test the country's energy supply too  with one power grid operator saying it's  taking steps to ensure supplies aren't  disrupted as people turn on their air  conditioning  here household energy bills in the uk  are likely to increase by around 2.7  billion pounds to cover the cost of the  28 energy supplies that have gone bust  in the last year and this doesn't take  into account the collapse of bulb energy  which might land another multi-billion  pound charge on consumers the national  auditor office has said that while the  failures were caused by the massive  changes in the energy market the  regulator of gem is also partly to blame  now europe needs to prepare for the  possibility of russia  cutting off its gas supplies completely  that's according to the head of the  international energy agency  fatty beryl's comments come as his  agency's report shows record amounts of  spending is going into clean energy  sources but not enough to plug the gap  in supply caused by the ukraine wall  we'll discuss all of this in a minute  but let's first take a look at the  details of this iea report investment in  clean energy was quite flat despite the  paris agreement in 2015 when countries  vowed to move away from 